<\body>
  <screens|<\hidden>
    <cqft-title-slide|<math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> on the torus II: a priori estimates and the limit|Joint work with Sarah-Jean Meyer, with the use of AI|SISSA lectures, Lecture<nbsp>7 \V notes: Lecture<nbsp>7, ŸŸ13\U18>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Notation from Lecture<nbsp>6>

    <\with|font-size|0.9>
      <\itemize>
        <item><strong|Lattice>: <math|\<Lambda\><rsub|N>=<around|(|N<rsup|-1>*\<bbb-Z\>/\<bbb-Z\>|)><rsup|3>>,
        <math|<big|int>f=N<rsup|-3>*<big|sum><rsub|x>f<around|(|x|)>>,
        <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<cdot\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p>> the
        <math|L<rsup|p>> norms, <math|\<Delta\>=\<Delta\><rsub|N>>.

        <item><strong|Free operators>: resolution
        <math|q\<in\><around|[|q<rsub|0>,\<infty\>|)>>,
        <math|K<rsub|q>=q-\<Delta\>> (precision),
        <math|C<rsub|q>=K<rsub|q><rsup|-1>> (unresolved covariance),
        <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>=q<rsup|-1>>;
        <math|C=C<rsub|q<rsub|0>>> the Gaussian reference,
        <math|K<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=q<rsub|0>-\<Delta\>> the free field
        operator, <math|q<rsub|0>\<gtr\>0> the reference mass.

        <item><strong|Couplings>: <math|U=<frac|\<lambda\>|12><big|int>\<varphi\><rsup|4>>, <math|g<rsub|q>=\<lambda\>*q<rsup|-1/2>>,
        <math|r<rsub|q>=<around|(|q/\<lambda\>|)><rsup|1/2>> (cube
        <math|\<asymp\>K<rsub|q>>), auxiliary scale
        <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>.

        <item><strong|Processes>: Polchinski process <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>>
        (<math|\<Phi\><rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0>), innovation <math|W>, bridge
        noise <math|B>, forecast <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>,
        <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>> its conditional covariance,
        filtration <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>=\<sigma\><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|s>:s\<leqslant\>q|)>>;
        <math|\<psi\><rsub|q>=m<rsub|q><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>>,
        <math|Y<rsub|q>=y<rsub|q><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>>.

        <item><strong|Forces>: exact <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>>
        (<math|K<rsub|q>\<Phi\><rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>\<psi\><rsub|q>+\<cal-F\><rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>|)>>),
        explicit <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=<frac|\<lambda\>|3>*y<rsup|3>+M<rsub|q>*y+<wide|\<ell\>|~><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>>;
        response <math|H<rsub|q>=<around|(|K<rsub|q>+D<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>|)><rsup|-1>>,
        defect drift <math|b<rsub|q>>,
        <math|\<Xi\><rsub|q>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|q>-H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>,
        coordinate correction <math|\<varrho\><rsub|q>>.
      </itemize>
    </with>
  </hidden>|<\shown>
    <mytitle|Where we are>

    At each lattice <math|N>, exactly (Lecture<nbsp>6):

    <\equation*>
      <\aligned>
        <tformat|<table|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>Y<rsub|q>>|<cell|=<around*|[|b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>*Z<rsub|q>|]>\<mathd\>q+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,>>|<row|<cell|\<mathd\>Z<rsub|q>>|<cell|=-<around*|[|b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>+H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>*Z<rsub|q>|]>\<mathd\>q+\<Xi\><rsub|q><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W<rsub|q>,>>>>
      </aligned>

      <space|2em>\<psi\>=Y+Z=<big|int><math-up|Cov><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>W.
    </equation*>

    <with|font-series|bold||Inputs, for |<math|q\<geqslant\>q<rsub|\<ast\>>>|.|>

    <\enumerate-Alpha>
      <item>Coercivity: <math|H<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>\<asymp\><around|(|K<rsub|q>+\<lambda\>*y<rsup|2>|)><rsup|-1>>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|H<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>\<leqslant\><around|(|1+\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>|)>/q>,
      <math|\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>=C*<around|(|g<rsub|q>+g<rsub|q><rsup|2>|)>>.

      <item>Source: <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|b>><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\><rsup|7/2>*q<rsup|-5/2>>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|b<rsup|<math-up|l>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C*\<lambda\><rsup|4>*q<rsup|-5><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|q><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>>.

      <item>Coordinates: <math|K<rsub|q>*<around|(|Y<rsub|q>+\<varrho\><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>|)>+<frac|\<lambda\>|3>*Y<rsub|q><rsup|3>=K<rsub|q>\<Phi\><rsub|q>>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|D*\<varrho\><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|p\<to\>p>\<leqslant\>\<varepsilon\><rsub|q>>.
    </enumerate-Alpha>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Goal>

      <math|Z> small and <math|Y> rarely at large fields, <strong|uniformly
      in <math|N>>. Neither equation can be estimated alone.
    </myframed>
  </shown>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|How FBSDE estimates usually work, and why not here>

    \;

    <with|font-series|bold|Usual> (Ma\UYong): Lipschitz coefficients, Itô on
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|Z|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>> and Gronwall; close on a
    short interval or under monotonicity.

    <with|font-series|bold|Here:> infinite interval, coefficients not
    uniformly Lipschitz, the coupling is not small. Instead we use
    <strong|structure>:

    <\itemize>
      <item>the backward equation is <strong|linear in <math|Z>> given
      <math|Y>: a conditional representation with an explicit propagator;

      <item>the forward equation is, by the field equation of Lecture 5, a
      cubic <strong|elliptic> equation at each resolution: coercive,
      estimated <strong|pathwise>;

      <item>the two estimates are linked by two weighted energies with
      <strong|matched weights>.
    </itemize>

    At fixed <math|N,T> all moments are finite (finite dimensions): used only
    to justify absorptions. <small|<with|color|dark grey|Ma\UYong 1999>>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <cqft-part-title|The energies>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture<nbsp>7>

