  </script-output|<image|<tuple|<#>|ps>|0.748par|||>>>

    The smallness is in the <strong|feedback>, independent of <math|p>: one
    <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> for all <math|p>; the growth <math|p<rsup|5>> is
    inherited from <math|G<rsub|T>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Consequences>

    \;

    Once the boundary term is removed (Section<nbsp>16):

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|N><around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>+sup<rsub|N><around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|1+p<rsup|5>|)>,<space|2em>sup<rsub|N>
      \<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsup|N><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>*q<rsup|-5/2>,
    </equation*>

    and <math|\<bbb-E\>*exp <around|{|c*<around|(|E<rsub|\<infty\>>+J<rsub|\<infty\>>|)><rsup|1/5>|}>\<less\>\<infty\>>.

    <\itemize>
      <item>The approximate forecast differs from the exact one by
      <math|O<around|(|q<rsup|-5/4>|)>> in
      <math|L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-P\>;L<rsup|2>|)>> (root mean square),
      uniformly in the lattice.

      <item><math|H<rsub|q>*Z<rsub|q>=O<around|(|q<rsup|-9/4>|)>> in
      <math|L<rsup|2>*<around|(|\<bbb-P\>;L<rsup|2>|)>>: up to an integrable
      drift, the forward equation is the explicit SDE
      <math|\<mathd\>Y=b<around|(|Y|)>\<mathd\>q+H<around|(|Y|)>\<mathd\>W>.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Comparison with the variational method>

    \;

    <\myframed>
      <strong|Comparison with Boué\UDupuis (Lecture<nbsp>4)>

      There: drift <math|u=-<frac|\<lambda\>|3>*<wide|Q|\<dot\>><rsup|1/2>:X<rsup|3>:+r> (<math|<frac|\<lambda\>|12>*<big|int>:\<varphi\><rsup|4>>: in Lecture<nbsp>4).
      Here: <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>>> does all such changes of
      variables at once in the forecast coordinate; <math|Z> plays the role
      of <math|r>. Coercivity of the quartic: <strong|pathwise> in the
      forward equation; the remainder: <strong|in expectation> through the
      backward one.
    </myframed>

    <with|par-mode|right|<small|<with|color|dark grey|Barashkov\UG. 2020>>>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Removing the cutoffs>

    \;

    So far, at every lattice <math|N> and upper scale <math|T>:

    <\equation*>
      <around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around*|(|1+p<rsup|5>+\<delta\><rsub|N,T,p>|)>,<space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|N,T,p>=<around|\<\|\|\>|T*Z<rsub|T>|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2><rsub|L<rsup|2*p><around|(|\<bbb-P\>;L<rsup|2>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    Three things remain:

    <\enumerate>
      <item>the boundary term vanishes: <math|T*Z<rsub|T>\<to\>0> at fixed
      <math|N>;

      <item>uniform energy bounds <math|\<Rightarrow\>> tightness of the
      measures;

      <item>identify the limit: coefficients, the explicit density, then the
      equations.
    </enumerate>

    <\myframed>
      <strong|The subtle point>

      Convergence of the fields does not pass an FBSDE to the limit: the
      backward martingale could acquire <strong|extra noise>. Here the limit
      is a density over an explicit flow, and the equations are read off from
      it.
    </myframed>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <cqft-part-title|The boundary at infinite resolution>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture<nbsp>7>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The boundary lemma>

    \;

    <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> was built to converge to the bare
    force: <math|\<mu\><rsub|q>\<to\>\<mu\><rsub|N>>,
    <math|\<Theta\><rsub|q>\<to\>0>, corrections integrated from
    <math|q=\<infty\>>.

    <\lemma*>
      <dueto|fixed <math|N>, constants depend on <math|N>>

      <\equation*>
        <around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|q>-\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|q*Z<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|N,p>*<space|0.17em>q<rsup|-1/2>,<space|2em><text|hence><space|2em>\<delta\><rsub|N,T,p>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|N,p>*T<rsup|-1>.
      </equation*>
    </lemma*>

    <\itemize>
      <item>The field at resolution <math|q>:
      <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=<around|(|q-q<rsub|0>|)>*C<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>+C<rsub|q>*B<rsub|q-q<rsub|0>>\<to\>\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>>.

      <item><math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=Y<rsub|q>+C<rsub|q>*<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>,
      so <math|Y<rsub|q>-\<Phi\><rsub|q>=O<around|(|q<rsup|-1>|)>> and
      <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>\<to\>D*V<rsub|N><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>>.

      <item>Exact minus approximate coordinate identity:

      <\equation*>
        q*Z<rsub|q>=q*C<rsub|q>*<around*|[|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>-\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*D*V<rsub|N><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>;
      </equation*>

      compare <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*G> with the <strong|observed>
      <math|G<rsup|0><rsub|q>=D*V<rsub|N><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>>:
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*G-G|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>2*<around|\<\|\|\>|G-G<rsup|0><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>>>.
    </itemize>

    No uniform estimate, no posterior concentration.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|UV stability>

    \;

    Let <math|T\<to\>\<infty\>> at fixed <math|N> (paths and
    <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>> do not depend on <math|T>; monotone convergence;
    <math|\<delta\><rsub|N,T,p>\<to\>0>). <strong|Only then> take
    <math|sup<rsub|N>>.

    <\theorem*>
      <dueto|UV stability>For <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>=q<rsub|\<ast\>><around|(|\<lambda\>|)>>
      large,

      <\equation*>
        sup<rsub|N><around*|(|<around|\<\|\|\>|E<rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>+<around|\<\|\|\>|J<rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>|)>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|1+p<rsup|5>|)>,<space|2em>sup<rsub|N>
        \<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsup|N><rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>*q<rsup|-5/2>,
      </equation*>

      and <math|sup<rsub|N> \<bbb-E\>*exp
      <around|{|c*<around|(|E<rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>+J<rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>|)><rsup|1/5>|}>\<less\>\<infty\>>.
    </theorem*>

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|C<rsub|N,p>> diverges with <math|N>: never take
      <math|sup<rsub|N>> of <math|C<rsub|N,p>/T>.

      <item>The force: <math|F<rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*<around|[|-D*V<rsub|N><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>|]>=K<rsub|q>*Z<rsub|q>-<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>,
      the explicit force at the approximate forecast, corrected by
      <math|K<rsub|q>*Z<rsub|q>>.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Restarted laws>

    \;

    The estimates use the start of the flow only through the noise. Let
    <math|\<bbb-P\><rsup|q,x>> be the exact flow conditioned on
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=x>: <math|\<Phi\><rsub|t>=C<rsub|t><around|(|K<rsub|q>x+<around|(|t-q|)><wide|\<Phi\>|^>+B<rprime|'><rsub|t-q>|)>>,
    with prior <math|\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|<around|\<langle\>|z,K<rsub|q>x|\<rangle\>>-<frac|1|2><around|\<langle\>|z,K<rsub|q>z|\<rangle\>>-V<rsub|N><around|(|z|)>><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    z>.

    The same posterior, the same system, and
    <math|\<zeta\><rsub|t>=C<rsub|t>K<rsub|q>x+\<zeta\><rsup|0><rsub|t>> with
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|t>K<rsub|q>x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\>2<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>.
    The field equation is <math|K<rsub|q>V<rsub|t>+<frac|\<lambda\>|3>Y<rsub|t><rsup|3>=<around|(|t-q|)><around|(|\<zeta\><rsub|t>-\<varrho\><rsub|t><around|(|Y<rsub|t>|)>+Z<rsub|t>|)>>.

    <strong|Restarted UV stability.> Uniformly in <math|N> and
    <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|x|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\> R>, the
    bounds on <math|E,J> and <math|\<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>
    C<rsub|R>s<rsup|-5/2>> hold on <math|<around|[|q\<vee\>
    q<rsub|\<ast\>>,\<infty\>|)>>.

    <strong|Below> <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>><strong|: deduced.> <math|\<psi\>>
    is a martingale, so <math|\<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\><rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2p>\<leqslant\>\<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|\<psi\><rsub|q<rsub|\<ast\>>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2p>>.
    The coordinate equation at <math|q<rsub|\<ast\>>> bounds
    <math|Y<rsub|s>>, and <math|Z<rsub|s>=\<psi\><rsub|s>-Y<rsub|s>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The role of the estimates>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Noise bound:> free-field tails for <math|\<zeta\>>,
      whatever the interaction.

      <item><strong|Forward coercivity:> the cube bounds large fields by
      noise tails and <math|Z>.

      <item><strong|Backward representation:> <math|Z> is the transported
      defect; <math|C\<lambda\><rsup|2>/q<rsub|\<ast\>>> closes the loop.

      <item><strong|Boundary:> at fixed <math|N>. Why the upper scale goes
      first.

      <item><strong|UV stability:> the control from the explicit to the exact
      flow has finite entropy.

      <item><strong|Restarted laws:> the same from any field at any scale;
      gives the kernels.

      <item><strong|Defect, Lipschitz coordinate:>
      <math|<big|int><around|\||e|\|>> integrable; the explicit flow is a
      strong SDE.

      <item><strong|Terminal tail:> tightness in <math|H<rsup|-s>>.
      <strong|Sources:> tails, end results only.
    </itemize>

    The formula for <math|\<nu\>> needs the first five at the start of the
    flow, with first moments. The FBSDE follows by Girsanov.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <cqft-part-title|Tightness>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture<nbsp>7>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Suprema by a maximum principle>

    Embed by cells <math|\<square\><rsub|x>>:
    <math|\<iota\><rsub|N>:H<rsub|N>\<to\>H=L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|3>|)>>
    isometric, <math|j<rsub|N>=\<iota\><rsub|N><rsup|\<ast\>>>.

    <\lemma*>
      <dueto|supremum after closure>Let <math|\<cal-Y\>=sup<rsub|q>q<rsup|-5/12>*<around|(|log
      <around|(|2+q|)>|)><rsup|-1/6><around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>,
      <math|\<cal-Z\>=sup<rsub|q>q<rsup|5/4><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>>.
      Uniformly in <math|N>,

      <\equation*>
        \<cal-Y\>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|1+\<cal-N\>+\<cal-Z\>|)>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-N\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<leqslant\>C<sqrt|p>,<space|2em><around|\<\|\|\>|\<cal-Z\><rsup|2>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|p>>\<leqslant\>C<rsub|\<lambda\>>*<around|(|1+p<rsup|5>|)>.
      </equation*>
    </lemma*>

    <\itemize>
      <item>From the <math|L<rsup|\<infty\>>> coercivity above:
      <strong|lattice maximum principle> in the field equation, no memory.

      <item><math|\<cal-N\>>: supremum of the noise (convex domination,
      Dudley, Doob). <math|\<cal-Z\>>: Doob on
      <math|\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*A<rsup|<math-up|l>>>, after the boundary term
      is removed.

      <item>The exponent <math|5/12\<less\><frac|1|2>> keeps <math|Y> below
      the threshold.
    </itemize>

    Finite-resolution suprema have all moments, uniformly in <math|N>. At the
    terminal resolution there is no such bound:
    <math|\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>> is a distribution.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The terminal field>

    \;

    Embed by cells: <math|\<iota\><rsub|N>:H<rsub|N>\<to\>
    H=L<rsup|2><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|3>|)>> isometric,
    <math|j<rsub|N>=\<iota\><rsub|N><rsup|\<ast\>>>.

    <math|H<rsub|q><rsup|2>\<leqslant\> C*q*C<rsub|q>> gives, for
    <math|<frac|1|2>\<less\>*s\<less\><frac|3|2>>,

    <\equation*>
      <big|int><rsub|Q><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\<\|\|\>|<around|(|1-\<Delta\>|)><rsup|-s/2>H<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|<math-up|HS>><rsup|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
      q\<leqslant\> C<big|int><rsub|Q><rsup|\<infty\>><frac|1|q><big|sum><rsub|k><frac|<around|(|1+<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>|)><rsup|-s>|q+c<around|\||k|\|><rsup|2>><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
      q\<leqslant\> C<rsub|s>Q<rsup|-<around*|(|s-<frac|1|2>|)>>,
    </equation*>

    and the drifts have squared tails <math|O<around|(|Q<rsup|-3>|)>>,
    <math|O<around|(|Q<rsup|-5/2>|)>>, <math|O<around|(|Q<rsup|-3/2>|)>>.
    Doob:

    <\equation*>
      sup<rsub|N>\<bbb-E\>sup<rsub|t\<geqslant\>
      Q><around|\<\|\|\>|Y<rsub|t>-Y<rsub|Q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|H<rsup|-s>><rsup|2>\<leqslant\>
      C<rsub|s>Q<rsup|-<around*|(|s-<frac|1|2>|)>>.
    </equation*>

    <math|\<nu\><rsub|N>> is tight in <math|H<rsup|-s><around|(|\<bbb-T\><rsup|3>|)>>
    for every <math|s\<gtr\><frac|1|2>>, with moments uniform in <math|N>.
    (Threshold of the free field in <math|d=3>.)
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <vspace*|2.5cm>

    \;

    <cqft-part-title|Identifying the limit>

    <vspace|0.3cm>

    <with|color|dark grey|Lecture<nbsp>7>

    \;
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The deterministic coefficients>

    \;

    Uniform bounds do not identify a limit: a weakly convergent vertex cannot
    in general be contracted against a varying covariance. Three tools:

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Weak vertex measures>: bounded rooted norms
      <math|\<Rightarrow\>> signed measures, weakly compact; the centred
      kernel in a first-moment topology. The limits solve the same fixed
      point, unique in its ball: the whole sequence converges.

      <item><strong|Compact sandwich>: <math|G<rsub|N>\<to\>G> in
      <math|<math-up|HS>>, <math|<math-ss|B><rsub|N>\<to\><math-ss|B>>
      weakly, symmetric, uniformly bounded,
      <math|sup<rsub|N><around|\<\|\|\>|G<rsub|N><math-ss|B><rsub|N>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>\<less\>1>,
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|G<math-ss|B>|\<\|\|\>>\<leqslant\>\<theta\>>
      <math|\<Rightarrow\>> <math|<around|(|I+G<rsub|N><math-ss|B><rsub|N>|)><rsup|-1>*G<rsub|N>\<to\><around|(|I+G<math-ss|B>|)><rsup|-1>*G>
      in <math|<math-up|HS>>.

      <item><strong|Contraction against a moving covariance>: rows of the
      inverses are Hölder <math|+> HS convergence <math|\<Rightarrow\>>
      <math|<around|(|H<rsup|N>|)><rsup|2>\<to\>H<rsup|2>> uniformly; weak
      vertices can be contracted.
    </itemize>

    Hence <math|H<rsup|N><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsup|N><rsub|q>|)>\<to\>H<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>
    in <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|I;<math-up|HS>|)>> and
    <math|b<rsup|N><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsup|N><rsub|q>|)>\<to\>b<rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>
    in <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|I;H|)>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The exact flow as a controlled explicit flow>

    \;

    <math|K<rsub|q>y+<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|y|)>=K<rsub|q>x>,
    <math|<wide|f|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|x|)>=K<rsub|q><around|(|y-x|)>>:
    the explicit force solves Burgers with a source
    <math|-K<rsub|q>H<rsub|q>\<cal-D\><rsub|q>\<circ\> y>.

    <strong|The first scalar.> <math|\<cal-D\>> and <math|<wide|f|\<bar\>>>
    are gradients: <math|e<rsub|q>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|1><around|\<langle\>|\<cal-D\><rsub|q><around|(|t*y|)>,y|\<rangle\>><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    t>, <math|D<wide|V|\<bar\>>=-<wide|f|\<bar\>>>, and

    <\equation*>
      \<partial\><rsub|q><wide|V|\<bar\>>+<frac|1|2><math-up|Tr><around|(|C<rsub|q><rsup|2>D<rsup|2><wide|V|\<bar\>>|)>-<frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|C<rsub|q>D<wide|V|\<bar\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>=e<rsub|q>\<circ\>
      y+c<around|(|q|)>.
    </equation*>

    Explicit flow <math|\<bbb-R\><rsup|q,x>>: <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    x=-C<rsub|s><around|(|x-y<rsub|s><around|(|x|)>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    s+C<rsub|s><space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W>, whose coordinate solves
    <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> Y=b<around|(|Y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    s+H<around|(|Y|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W>. It contains no <math|Z>.

    <math|y<rsub|s>> is Lipschitz for the uniform norm (maximum principle),
    uniformly in <math|N>: a strong continuum SDE.

    <strong|The exact flow is a controlled explicit flow.>
    <math|\<Delta\>=V-<wide|V|\<bar\>>> solves a Hamilton\UJacobi\UBellman
    equation with control <math|u>, drift <math|C<rsub|s>u<rsub|s>>, and
    running cost <math|e+c+<frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|u|\<\|\|\>><rsup|2>>.
    Its optimal feedback is <math|-C<rsub|s>D\<Delta\><rsub|s>=Z<rsub|s>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Density and sandwich>

    \;

    With <math|A<rsub|N><around|(|q,x|)>=V<rsub|q><around|(|x|)>-<wide|V|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|x|)>-<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>c>,
    at fixed <math|N>:

    <\equation*>
      <frac|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<bbb-P\><rsup|q,x>|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<bbb-R\><rsup|q,x>>=\<mathe\><rsup|A<rsub|N>>exp<around|(|-<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>e<rsub|s><around|(|Y<rsub|s>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
      s|)>,<space|1em><math-up|Ent><around|(|\<bbb-P\><rsup|q,x>\|\<bbb-R\><rsup|q,x>|)>=<frac|1|2><big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>\<bbb-E\><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
      s,
    </equation*>

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|N>=<math-up|Ent>+\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><big|int>
      e,<space|1em><text|so><space|1em>\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-P\>><big|int>
      e\<leqslant\> A<rsub|N>\<leqslant\>\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-R\>><big|int>
      e.
    </equation*>

    The defect on all fields: <math|<around|\||e<rsub|s><around|(|y|)>|\|>\<leqslant\>
    C\<lambda\><rsup|5>s<rsup|-3><around|\<\|\|\>|\<pi\><rsub|s>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|4><rsup|4>+C\<lambda\><rsup|4>s<rsup|-2><around|\<\|\|\>|\<pi\><rsub|s>y|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>+C\<lambda\><rsup|3>s<rsup|-5/2><around|\<\|\|\>|t<rsub|s><around|(|y|)>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|6><rsup|6>>.
    So both sides are bounded uniformly in <math|N>, by the energies under
    <math|\<bbb-P\>> and by Itô under <math|\<bbb-R\>>.

    <strong|No penalty, no sub- or supersolutions, no exponential moment of
    the defect:> only expectations of <math|e>, and the entropy of the
    control.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The limit law>

    \;

    Let <math|\<iota\><rsub|N>x<rsub|N>\<to\> x> uniformly. Couple the flows
    by the same <math|W>: paths converge uniformly, <math|<big|int>
    e<rsup|N>\<to\><big|int> e> in probability. <strong|The entropy bound
    makes the densities uniformly integrable>, so they converge in
    <math|L<rsup|1>>:

    <\equation*>
      \<Pi\><rsub|q>O<around|(|x|)>=\<mathe\><rsup|A<around|(|q,x|)>>\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-R\><rsup|q,x>><around*|[|O<around|(|Y<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>e>|]>,<space|1em>\<mathe\><rsup|-A<around|(|q,x|)>>=\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-R\><rsup|q,x>>\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int><rsub|q><rsup|\<infty\>>e>,
    </equation*>

    jointly continuous in <math|<around|(|q,x|)>>.

    <strong|The measure> is the kernel at <math|<around|(|q<rsub|0>,0|)>>.
    With <math|\<bbb-R\>> the flow from <math|Y<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0>, along the
    whole sequence,

    <\equation*>
      <big|int> O<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<nu\>=<frac|\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-R\>><around*|[|O<around|(|Y<rsub|\<infty\>>|)>\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|\<infty\>>e>|]>|\<bbb-E\><rsup|\<bbb-R\>><around*|[|\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|\<infty\>>e>|]>>,<space|1em>-log<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|N>><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<mu\><rsub|C>-\<cal-C\><rsub|N><around|(|q<rsub|0>|)>\<to\>
      A<around|(|q<rsub|0>,0|)>.
    </equation*>

    As in Gubinelli\UMeyer for sine\UGordon, but the defect is unbounded: the
    entropy replaces the bound.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The filtering model and the FBSDE>

    \;

    Under <math|\<bbb-P\>=f\<bbb-R\>>, with
    <math|f=\<mathe\><rsup|A<around|(|q<rsub|0>,0|)>>\<mathe\><rsup|-<big|int>
    e>>, <math|\<Phi\>=Y<rsub|\<infty\>>>,
    <math|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>=\<sigma\><around|(|x<rsub|s>:s\<leqslant\> q|)>>:

    <\itemize>
      <item><math|\<Phi\>\<sim\>\<nu\>>, <math|x<rsub|q>=<around|(|q-q<rsub|0>|)>C<rsub|q>\<Phi\>+C<rsub|q>B<rsub|q-q<rsub|0>>>
      with <math|B> independent of <math|\<Phi\>>;

      <item><math|\<psi\><rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|\<Phi\>\|\<cal-F\><rsub|q>|]>=m<rsub|q><around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>>,
      a continuous function of the field;

      <item><math|W=P-<big|int>\<psi\>> is a Brownian motion, and with
      <math|Y=y<around|(|x|)>>, <math|Z=\<psi\>-Y>, the FBSDE holds from
      <math|Y<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=Z<rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0>;

      <item><math|\<psi\>=<big|int><math-up|Cov><space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W>,
      no orthogonal part, <math|<math-up|Cov>> the conditional covariance;

      <item>the lattice processes converge in law.
    </itemize>

    <strong|Proof.> <math|\<cal-F\>> is Brownian under <math|\<bbb-R\>>. So
    <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> D=D<around|\<langle\>|Z<rprime|'>,<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    W<rsup|\<bbb-R\>>|\<rangle\>>>, and Girsanov gives
    <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> P=<around|(|Y+Z<rprime|'>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    s+<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W>. By independence of <math|B> the drift is
    <math|\<psi\>>. For <math|M=N/D>, Itô gives <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    M=<around|\<langle\>|n/D-M*Z,<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W|\<rangle\>>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Hidden noise>

    \;

    <math|L<rsup|n><rsub|t>=<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>g<rsub|n><around|(|s|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>B<rsub|s>>,
    <math|g<rsub|n>=1+sin <around|(|n*s|)>>: integrands <math|\<to\>1>
    weakly, squares <math|\<to\><frac|3|2>>.

    <\equation*>
      <around|(|L<rsup|n>,B|)>\<Longrightarrow\><around|(|B+2<rsup|-1/2>*<wide|B|~>,<space|0.27em>B|)>,<space|2em><wide|B|~>\<perp\>B.
    </equation*>

    <image|<tuple|<#>|hidden_noise.pdf>|0.6par|||>

    Left: <math|<around|(|B<rsub|1>,L<rsup|n><rsub|1>-B<rsub|1>|)>>,
    <math|n=200>: correlation <math|\<approx\>0>, variance of the gap
    <math|\<approx\><frac|1|2>> \V the residual does not vanish and is
    orthogonal to <math|B>. Right: <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>g<rsub|n>\<to\>t>
    but <math|<big|int><rsub|0><rsup|t>g<rsub|n><rsup|2>\<to\><frac|3|2>*t>.

    A limit by compactness could carry an extra orthogonal martingale. Here
    the density excludes it: the filtration is Brownian under the reference.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The renormalised field equation>

    \;

    <\theorem*>
      <dueto|integration by parts><math|G<around|(|\<varphi\>|)>=g<around|(|<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,h<rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>,\<ldots\>,<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,h<rsub|k>|\<rangle\>>|)>>,
      <math|g\<in\>C<rsub|b><rsup|\<infty\>>>, <math|h,h<rsub|i>> smooth:

      <\equation*>
        lim<rsub|N> \<bbb-E\>*<around*|[|G*<around|(|\<iota\><rsub|N>*\<Phi\><rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>|)><around|\<langle\>|\<mu\><rsub|N>*\<Phi\><rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>+<frac|\<lambda\>|3><around|(|\<Phi\><rsup|N><rsub|\<infty\>>|)><rsup|3>,j<rsub|N>*h|\<rangle\>>|]>
      </equation*>

      <\equation*>
        =\<bbb-E\><rsub|\<nu\>>*<around*|[|D<rsub|h>*G<around|(|\<Phi\>|)>-G<around|(|\<Phi\>|)><around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>,<around|(|q<rsub|0>-\<Delta\>|)>*h|\<rangle\>>|]>.
      </equation*>
    </theorem*>

    Proof: Gaussian integration by parts in
    <math|\<mathe\><rsup|-V<rsub|N>-<frac|1|2>*<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,K<rsub|q<rsub|0>>*\<varphi\>|\<rangle\>>>>;
    tightness and uniform moments.

    <\itemize>
      <item>The terminal case of the DS equations of Lecture<nbsp>5.

      <item>The renormalised cube <math|\<mu\><rsub|N>*\<Phi\>+<frac|\<lambda\>|3>*\<Phi\><rsup|3>>
      is identified only <strong|tested in expectation>, not as a random
      distribution.

      <item>Limiting force without terminal representative: <strong|not
      proved>.

      <item>At every finite resolution the force is explicit:
      <math|F<rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>*Z<rsub|q>-<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|The field equation at resolution <math|q>>

    In the limit: <math|\<omega\><rsub|q>=K<rsub|q>\<Phi\><rsub|q>-<around|(|q-q<rsub|0>|)>*\<psi\><rsub|q>>,
    <math|F<rsub|q>=K<rsub|q><around|(|\<psi\><rsub|q>-\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>>,
    <math|J<rsub|q>=<around|(|q-q<rsub|0>|)>*C<rsub|q>\<to\>I>.

    <\theorem*>
      For every <math|q>, with no renormalisation constant, pathwise as
      distributions,

      <\equation*>
        K<rsub|q<rsub|0>>*\<Phi\><rsub|q>=J<rsub|q>*F<rsub|q>+\<omega\><rsub|q>,<space|2em>\<bbb-E\>*D<rsub|J<rsub|q>*h>*G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|G<around|(|\<Phi\><rsub|q>|)><around|\<langle\>|\<omega\><rsub|q>,h|\<rangle\>>|]>.
      </equation*>

      <math|<around|\<langle\>|F,h|\<rangle\>>>,
      <math|<around|\<langle\>|\<omega\>,h|\<rangle\>>> are martingales, and
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|<around|\<langle\>|F<rsub|q>+<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>,h|\<rangle\>>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|L<rsup|2>>\<lesssim\><around|\<\|\|\>|h|\<\|\|\>><rsub|H<rsup|2>>*q<rsup|-1/4>>.
    </theorem*>

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|Proof of DS:> <math|\<omega\><rsub|q>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|q>*B<rsub|q-q<rsub|0>>>
      and <math|\<Phi\><rsub|q>=J<rsub|q>*\<Phi\><rsub|\<infty\>>+C<rsub|q>*B<rsub|q-q<rsub|0>>>:
      Gaussian integration by parts in <math|B>.

      <item><strong|The renormalised cube is an observable:> the unique
      martingale in the class of the explicit
      <math|-<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>.

      <item><math|q\<to\>\<infty\>> recovers the integration by parts
      formula. Not proved: that <math|F> is closed.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Quartic tails: the tilt>

    \;

    Hairer\USteele proved quartic tails by tilting the dynamics. Here: the
    filter is available for every prior, and the noise bound does not depend
    on the prior.

    Let <math|<wide|\<nu\>|^>\<propto\>\<mathe\><rsup|h<around|\<langle\>|\<varphi\>,f|\<rangle\>>>\<nu\><rsub|N>>.
    This is the flow restarted at <math|<around|(|q<rsub|0>,h*C<rsub|q<rsub|0>>f|)>>.
    <strong|Shift the coordinate:>

    <\equation*>
      K<rsub|q>Y<rsub|q>+<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>=K<rsub|q>\<Phi\><rsub|q>+h*f,<space|1em>
      Z<rsub|q>=<wide|\<psi\>|^><rsub|q>-Y<rsub|q>.
    </equation*>

    The system is the same, <math|T*Z<rsub|T>\<to\>0> at fixed <math|N>, and
    the field equation gains <math|h*f>: the noise gains
    <math|d<rsub|q>=h*C<rsub|q>f>, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|d<rsub|q>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>\<leqslant\><around|\||h|\|><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>/q>.

    So UV stability holds on <math|<around|[|q<rsub|h>,\<infty\>|)>> when
    <math|q<rsub|h><rsup|3/2>\<geqslant\>70\<lambda\><rsup|1/2><around|\||h|\|><around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>>:
    where the cube at its threshold, <math|q*r<rsub|q>>, dominates the
    source.

    An exact identity, with no cube and no derivative:
    <math|<wide|\<Phi\>|^>=Y<rsub|q>+Z<rsub|q>+<around|(|\<omega\><rsub|q>-B<rsub|q-q<rsub|0>>|)>/<around|(|q-q<rsub|0>|)>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Quartic tails: closing>

    \;

    Test the identity with <math|f>, <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<ast\>>=<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|1>+<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>+<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<infty\>>=1>,
    and average over <math|<around|[|q<rsub|h>,2q<rsub|h>|]>>,
    <math|X=<around|\<langle\>|<wide|\<Phi\>|^>,f|\<rangle\>>>:

    <\equation*>
      <wide|\<bbb-E\>|^><around|\||X|\|>\<leqslant\>
      C*r<rsub|q<rsub|h>>+C\<leqslant\> C<around|(|1+<around|\||h|\|>|)><rsup|1/3>.
    </equation*>

    As <math|L<around|(|h|)>=log\<bbb-E\>\<mathe\><rsup|h<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>,f|\<rangle\>>>>
    has <math|L<rprime|'><around|(|h|)>=<wide|\<bbb-E\>|^>X>, we get
    <math|L<around|(|h|)>\<leqslant\> C<around|(|<around|\||h|\|>+<around|\||h|\|><rsup|4/3>|)>>.
    Chernoff:

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\>exp<around|(|\<beta\><around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\>,f|\<rangle\>><rsup|4>/<around|\<\|\|\>|f|\<\|\|\>><rsub|\<ast\>><rsup|4>|)>\<leqslant\>
      C,<space|1em>sup<rsub|N>\<bbb-E\>exp<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|\<rho\>><around|\<\|\|\>|\<iota\><rsub|N>\<Phi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|H<rsup|-\<rho\>>><rsup|4>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>,<space|1em>\<rho\>\<gtr\><frac|3|2>.
    </equation*>

    <strong|Gaussian tails at the free threshold.> A quadratic tilt
    <math|W=<frac|\<beta\>|2><around|\<\|\|\>|\<iota\><rsub|N>\<varphi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|H<rsup|-s>><rsup|2>>,
    with boundary term <math|D*W>, gives <math|sup<rsub|N>\<bbb-E\>exp<around|(|\<beta\><rsub|s><around|\<\|\|\>|\<iota\><rsub|N>\<Phi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|H<rsup|-s>><rsup|2>|)>\<less\>\<infty\>>,
    <math|s\<gtr\><frac|1|2>>.

    The quartic moment at <math|s> near <math|<frac|1|2>> would need
    spatially resolved sources.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Dyson\USchwinger along the scales>

    \;

    <strong|DS along the scales.> For <math|G=g<around|(|<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|q<rsub|1>>,h<rsub|1>|\<rangle\>>,\<ldots\>,<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|q<rsub|k>>,h<rsub|k>|\<rangle\>>|)>>,
    <math|q<rsub|i>\<leqslant\> q>:

    <\equation*>
      \<bbb-E\><big|sum><rsub|i>\<partial\><rsub|i>g<around|\<langle\>|J<rsub|q<rsub|i>>h,h<rsub|i>|\<rangle\>>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|G<around|\<langle\>|\<omega\><rsub|q>,h|\<rangle\>>|]>=\<bbb-E\><around|[|G<around|(|<around|\<langle\>|\<Phi\><rsub|q>,K<rsub|q<rsub|0>>h|\<rangle\>>+<around|\<langle\>|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>,J<rsub|q>h|\<rangle\>>|)>|]>+O<around|(|q<rsup|-1/4>|)>.
    </equation*>

    So the explicit force <math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>
    closes the hierarchy at large <math|q>, up to an error which vanishes as
    <math|q\<to\>\<infty\>>.
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Uniqueness in law and the variational formula>

    \;

    <strong|Scalar form.> <math|A<rsub|t>=A<around|(|t,x<rsub|t>|)>> solves

    <\equation*>
      A<rsub|t>=<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|e<rsub|s><around|(|Y<rsub|s>|)>+<frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|Z<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
      s+<big|int><rsub|t><rsup|\<infty\>><around|\<langle\>|Z<rsub|s>,<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
      W<rsub|s>|\<rangle\>>.
    </equation*>

    With the forward equation <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    P=<around|(|Y+Z|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\> s+<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W>
    this has <strong|uniqueness in law>: Girsanov removes <math|Z>, pathwise
    uniqueness gives <math|\<bbb-R\>>, and the scalar equation gives the
    density. <math|Z> is the feedback <math|m<around|(|x|)>-y<around|(|x|)>>.

    <strong|Renormalised variational formula.> The Gibbs principle over
    <math|\<bbb-R\>>:

    <\equation*>
      -log<big|int>\<mathe\><rsup|-O><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>\<nu\>=\<cal-G\><around|(|O|)>-\<cal-G\><around|(|0|)>,<space|1em>\<cal-G\><around|(|O|)>=inf<rsub|u>\<bbb-E\><around*|[|O<around|(|Y<rsup|u><rsub|\<infty\>>|)>+<big|int><rsub|q<rsub|0>><rsup|\<infty\>><around*|(|e<rsub|s><around|(|Y<rsup|u><rsub|s>|)>+<frac|1|2><around|\<\|\|\>|u<rsub|s>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2><rsup|2>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
      s|]>,
    </equation*>

    <math|<space|0.17em>\<mathd\> Y<rsup|u>=<around|(|b<around|(|Y<rsup|u>|)>+H<around|(|Y<rsup|u>|)>u|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\>
    s+H<around|(|Y<rsup|u>|)><space|0.17em>\<mathd\> W>,
    <math|Y<rsup|u><rsub|q<rsub|0>>=0>. Minimiser for <math|O=0>: <math|u=Z>.
    <strong|No infinite constant, no initial law to optimise.>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|What is not proved>

    \;

    <\itemize>
      <item><strong|The vector FBSDE alone:> <math|Z> need not be a gradient.
      It would suffice that any solution has a Lipschitz decoupling field: a
      pointwise bound on <math|<math-up|Cov><rsub|q><around|(|x|)>>, or
      high-scale contraction with non-degeneracy. Only
      <math|L<rsup|2><around|(|<math-up|HS>|)>> control is available.

      <item><strong|Strong solutions:> is the Polchinski process adapted to
      <math|W>? The same bound. Allinger\UMitter does not apply, as
      <math|<around|\<\|\|\>|\<Phi\>|\<\|\|\>><rsub|2>=\<infty\>>.

      <item><strong|Tails:> the quartic moment at <math|s> near
      <math|<frac|1|2>>.

      <item><strong|Axioms:> reflection positivity holds on the lattice and
      passes to <math|\<nu\>> for every coordinate reflection (standard;
      exercise); rotation invariance and clustering need infinite volume.

      <item><strong|Infinite volume:> the force and its Burgers flow are
      intensive, but the entropy and the defect are extensive, so estimates
      must be made local. The noise from infinity in the nonlocal
      <math|\<Xi\>> must also be controlled.
    </itemize>
  </hidden>|<\hidden>
    <mytitle|Summary of the course>

    \;

    Wightman <math|\<to\>> Euclidean (L1) <math|\<to\>> free field (L2)
    <math|\<to\>> stochastic quantisation, scale decomposition (L3)
    <math|\<to\>> Polchinski, FBSDE, variational problem (L4) <math|\<to\>> field equation at every resolution, sub-Gaussian noise (L5) <math|\<to\>> <math|\<Phi\><rsup|4><rsub|3>> through the FBSDE in forecast coordinates
    (L6\U7).

    <data-table|<tformat|<table|<row|<cell|Lecture
    4 (formal)>|<cell|Lectures 6\U7>|<cell|in the limit>>|<row|<cell|forecast
    <math|\<psi\><rsub|t>=\<bbb-E\><rsub|t>*\<Phi\><rsub|T>>>|<cell|<math|\<psi\>=Y+Z>>|<cell|closed
    martingale>>|<row|<cell|response <math|H<rsub|t>=<math-up|Cov><rsub|t>>>|<cell|<math|<math-up|Cov>=H<around|(|Y|)>+\<Xi\>>>|<cell|conditional
    covariance>>|<row|<cell|<math|\<cal-F\><rsub|t>>, force
    equation>|<cell|<math|<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q>> explicit, defect
    <math|\<cal-D\>>>|<cell|deterministic coefficients>>|<row|<cell|force
    <math|F<rsub|t>>>|<cell|<math|K<rsub|q>*Z<rsub|q>-<wide|\<cal-F\>|\<bar\>><rsub|q><around|(|Y<rsub|q>|)>>>|<cell|observable
    at finite <math|q>>>|<row|<cell|DS equation>|<cell|integration by
    parts>|<cell|tested in expectation>>>>>

    <strong|UV stability> <math|=> an explicit force predicts the conditional
    mean up to a small error. So the measure is an explicit density over an
    explicit flow, with that error as the optimal control.
  </hidden>>
</body>

